{"title":"2026 AIME I Problems/Problem 3","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Sol_1_+_Diagram)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Sol 1 + Diagram)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_(Fast_and_Easy_🔥🚀)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution (Fast and Easy 🔥🚀)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_MadMath\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by MadMath</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A hemisphere with radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/3/8f37626d49b4082c9102bf930bca51277ff2eb43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$200$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> sits on top of a horizontal circular disk with radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/3/8f37626d49b4082c9102bf930bca51277ff2eb43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$200$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" />, and the hemisphere and disk have the same center. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/3/26394ab97bace60c3c2285deb5a7c93ac72631b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> be the region of points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> in the disk such that a sphere of radius 42 can be placed on top of the disk at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and lie completely inside the hemisphere. The area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/3/26394ab97bace60c3c2285deb5a7c93ac72631b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> divided by the area of the disk is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/4/7347a1d5586e82c832f61ea799d89945ed2837fc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{p}{q}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"11\" height=\"36\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> are relatively prime positive integers. Find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/0/170200756543596efeb46fca1cf29a617e8c9192.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p+q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"41\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/3/26394ab97bace60c3c2285deb5a7c93ac72631b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> will be the shape of a circle by symmetry. To find the radius, imagine the case in which the sphere is in contact with the very edge of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/3/26394ab97bace60c3c2285deb5a7c93ac72631b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />. Let the center of the sphere be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />, and the center of the hemisphere be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/9/b1902d279ba37d60bdce4e0e987b7cd19d48974e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />. Imagine the vertical plane containing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/9/b1902d279ba37d60bdce4e0e987b7cd19d48974e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p><br />\nOn the plane, a right triangle exists with vertices <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/9/b1902d279ba37d60bdce4e0e987b7cd19d48974e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> (the point of tangency between sphere and disk). We know <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/0/9e0460c16d8b6a426cb23b2e82b1472ee8f46bb2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$SH$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/7/0c7a280c250885294f8696d4f42282f735ef8f09.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$200 - 42 = 158$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"121\" height=\"15\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/1/4b101ae280775cd91fd7bb1d46e315a73a11ff5f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$SP$\" width=\"26\" height=\"12\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/0/ed0d6c36fdad9c2874053be46c7e4120f2edcf47.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$42$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" />, the radius of the sphere. \n</p><p><br />\nNow we can use the Pythagorean theorem to get the radius of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/3/26394ab97bace60c3c2285deb5a7c93ac72631b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/4/8d4ccaae15360ec1c98ad4bc0ed340ba1524b0ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\sqrt{5800}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"62\" height=\"21\" />. Finding the area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/3/26394ab97bace60c3c2285deb5a7c93ac72631b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and dividing by the area of the disk will give you a final answer of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/2/8c2de22a0a267423747cf862014e201c6d9861e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{29}{50}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"24\" height=\"38\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/3/603da3e284d416ad56a2ab0eabd3c9179fb3c9c0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{079}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"39\" height=\"23\" />.\n</p><p><br />\nNote: Using the Pythagorean theorem on big numbers can be tricky, so we can scale down the triangle by a factor of two and rescale it when we find the third side.\n</p><p><br />\n~ <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Logibyte\" title=\"Logibyte\">Logibyte</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>By symmetry, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/3/26394ab97bace60c3c2285deb5a7c93ac72631b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> is a disk. Consider the boundary case where the sphere of radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/0/ed0d6c36fdad9c2874053be46c7e4120f2edcf47.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$42$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" /> is internally tangent to the hemisphere. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/9/b1902d279ba37d60bdce4e0e987b7cd19d48974e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> be the center of the hemisphere, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> the point of tangency, and let line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/6/06695cb2d063998a9cf5c454b72813e6c3d7e735.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$HA$\" width=\"30\" height=\"13\" /> intersect the small sphere again at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be the point on the disk directly below the center of the small sphere.\n</p><p>Since the hemisphere has radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/3/8f37626d49b4082c9102bf930bca51277ff2eb43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$200$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/0/f20d445af83708701794c0874df5133f2b596753.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$HA=200$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"84\" height=\"14\" />. The diameter of the small sphere is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/3/4131457f67c5dbfd8b1c6be1f74a47595a9bd3be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$84$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"18\" height=\"13\" />,\nso <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/d/cddc5ef8a46e323e7a7cc5ee4e380c7bac125923.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$HM=200-84=116$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"181\" height=\"16\" />. By Power of a Point at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/9/b1902d279ba37d60bdce4e0e987b7cd19d48974e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/8/52820a76bee213395d5362705199d0e57218046d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$HP^2=HA\\cdot HM=200\\cdot116.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"241\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p>Thus the radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/3/26394ab97bace60c3c2285deb5a7c93ac72631b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> satisfies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/7/fc782d3582792842092c6881a3d2eeef62013ed9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r^2=200\\cdot116$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"110\" height=\"17\" />.\n</p><p>Our desired ratio is\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/5/185af146e6adcb1f1f8d5629263580f220b6875c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\pi r^2}{\\pi(200)^2}=\\frac{200\\cdot116}{200^2}=\\frac{29}{50}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"208\" height=\"45\" />. Therefore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/9/6693952b55117845b0a7094bf08150e33eadbca0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p+q=\\boxed{79}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"96\" height=\"25\" />.\n</p><p>~ MathKing555\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Sol_1_.2B_Diagram.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Sol_1_+_Diagram)\">Solution 3 (Sol 1 + Diagram)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Sol 1 + Diagram)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/6/f56810cd4839b55117d031d68cec50a6e85e8f38.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] size(8cm);  real R = 200; real r = 42; real r_T = sqrt(R * (R - 2*r));   // Outer circle (Base Disk / Hemisphere boundary) draw(circle((0,0), R)); label(&quot;P&quot;, (0, R), N);  // Inner circle (Region T boundary) draw(circle((0,0), r_T)); label(&quot;$\\mathcal{T}$&quot;, (0, r_T), N);  // Center point dot((0,0)); [/asy]\" width=\"358\" height=\"378\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/2/812a5bf65a623b4fb2fddedb8c99a2b981265d15.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{T}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> is the circle in which the centre of the sphere of radius 42 can be placed through rotational symmetry: When we revolve the circle along the hemisphere's point of tangency with the regular sphere inclusive with the circles point of tangency with the plane <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, we obtain a circle, as shown above.\n</p><p>Consider the cross section <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/f/74f4008768812f46db4b252c9e69cf98c5faaa4b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{P}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> of the plane consisting of the centre of both the Hemisphere and the circle. \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/c/7bce587fca01017cc6aea37ebc6dfc825b44efc9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy]  size(8cm);  real R = 200; real r = 42; real r_T = sqrt(R * (R - 2*r));   // Hemisphere cross-section path semicircle = arc((0,0), R, 0, 180); draw(semicircle);  // Base disk cross-section draw((-R,0)--(R,0));  // Region T on the base line draw((-r_T,0)--(r_T,0)); dot((r_T,0)); dot((-r_T,0));  // Boundary sphere cross-section pair C = (r_T, r); path small_circle = circle(C, r); draw(small_circle); dot(C);  // Radius from origin to boundary touchpoint real angle = atan2(r, r_T);  pair furthest_point = (R*cos(angle), R*sin(angle)); draw((0,0)--furthest_point); dot(furthest_point);  // Origin dot((0,0));  [/asy]\" width=\"378\" height=\"192\" />\n</p><p>We find the area of this region. We approach like in solution 1, using the Pythagorean Theorem and finding the radius of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/2/812a5bf65a623b4fb2fddedb8c99a2b981265d15.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{T}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/a/4aae9e85b41c74c34e13f860f9f0a034687721cd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20\\sqrt{58}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"53\" height=\"21\" />. The area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/2/812a5bf65a623b4fb2fddedb8c99a2b981265d15.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{T}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> is then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/d/b3dc6cef74220b5b0a959762e51595d28e262728.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$23200 \\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"58\" height=\"14\" /> and the area of the disk is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/2/c52768127ea318a4e308c20ee08e013daa3be238.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$40000\\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"58\" height=\"14\" /> so the ration is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/1/8112fbc428364490d1007f59f8c1082883251ef2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{232}{400} = \\frac{29}{50} \\implies \\boxed{79}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"158\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_.28Fast_and_Easy_.F0.9F.94.A5.F0.9F.9A.80.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_(Fast_and_Easy_🔥🚀)\">Video Solution (Fast and Easy 🔥🚀)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution (Fast and Easy 🔥🚀)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Rw235h4qHms\">2026 AIME I #3</a>\n</p><p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://piacademyus.org/\">By piacademyus.org</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YtrhwNWhFic\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YtrhwNWhFic</a> - Continuum Math\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_MadMath\">Video Solution by MadMath</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by MadMath\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/ceq1ruwyekQ\">https://youtu.be/ceq1ruwyekQ</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_I\" title=\"2026 AIME I\">2026 AIME I</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/i